# Who really pays a tax?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Does it matter whether the buyer or the seller hands the tax to the state? Why does the less flexible side end up paying more?"
next: {"title":"How much do buyers and sellers gain from a market?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/16-how-much-do-buyers-and-sellers-gain-from.cast"}

## Who hands it over
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Loaves a week (Q)
    y_label: Price of a loaf (P)
    demand:
      elasticity: 1
    supply:
      elasticity: 1
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
    tax:
      amount: 1
      side: seller
      label: S + tax
    regions:
      - government_revenue
    units:
      price: [0, 8]
      quantity: [0, 1000]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: loaves
    readout:
      - price_buyers
      - price_sellers
    readout_at: inside
sources:
    - id: irs751
      title: Topic no. 751, Social Security and Medicare withholding rates
      authors: Internal Revenue Service
      year: 2025
      finding: Social Security tax is 6.2% of wages for the employer and 6.2% for the employee (12.4% in all); Medicare 1.45% each
      url: https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc751

    scratch fica center cites ["irs751"] work ["US payroll tax","worker 6.2%","employer 6.2%"] font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "Who really pays a tax?"

Who really pays a tax? Take America's payroll tax  (@draw fica_box fica_line_1 fica_line_2@)for pensions: by law, the worker pays six point two percent of the wage, and the employer pa (@draw fica_line_3@)ys another six point two.

B: So Congress split it down the middle. Isn't that the answer, then?
    highlight fica_line_2 fica_line_3

Economists say the law only decides who writes the chequ (@draw axes@)e. Let's test that on a smaller case.
    erase fica

Mia's bakery in Harbourtown, with illustrative numbers: a loaf sell (@draw equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar@)s for four dollars, and about five hundred sell a week.
    draw demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S

B: Now the harbour council puts a tax of one dollar on every loaf, and Mia has to hand it over.
    highlight supply_curve label_S

    ask "What will a loaf cost, in dollars, with the $1 tax? Set your guess." estimate.min 4 estimate.max 5 estimate.value 4.5 store g stage own
        intro: Before anything moves: what will a loaf cost once the tax is in?
        right: Close: you said {g}, and it settles at {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it settles at {g.true}.

    erase estimate_1

To keep baking each loaf, Mia now needs a dollar more  (@animate tax.amount 12.5 duration 2.5@)for it, so her supply curve lifts by the whole dollar.
    draw tax_supply_curve label_S_tax

B: So the price jumps a dollar, to five?
    point at.data [50,62.5] gesture circle

No. At five dollars shoppers b (@erase label_Pstar@)uy fewer loaves, so the price settles in between: (@draw readout_price_buyers@) four dollars fifty.
    draw tax_equilibrium_point tax_guide_lines price_buyers_point label_Pb

Shoppers pay fifty cents more. Mia h (@draw wedge_region label_wedge@)ands the council a (@draw price_sellers_point label_Ps readout_price_sellers@) dollar and keeps three fifty, so she's fifty cents worse off too.

B: Half and half, then, even though the law put the whole dollar on Mia.
    highlight label_Pb label_Ps

That split is what economists call the tax's incidence: who ends up poorer, not who hands over the money.
    highlight readout effect box

    pause 0.5

## Who can walk away
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Loaves a week (Q)
    y_label: Price of a loaf (P)
    demand:
      elasticity: 1
    supply:
      elasticity: 1
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
    tax:
      amount: 0
      side: buyer
      label: D − tax
    units:
      price: [0, 8]
      quantity: [0, 1000]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: loaves
    readout:
      - price_buyers
      - price_sellers
    readout_at: inside

    card title "Who can walk away"

Mia hands over the dollar, yet shoppers pay half of it. What if the council  (@draw equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar@)sent the bill to the shoppers instead, at the till?
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S

    ask "The $1 is now collected from shoppers. Where does demand go, as Mia sees it? Move it, then press Done." on demand_curve predict true store t check size voice b
        intro: Then I'd predict the shoppers' curve moves. Try it yourself first.
        right: Yes. {t.why}
        wrong: Not quite. {t.why}

B: Seen from Mia's side, e (@animate tax.amount 12.5 duration 2.5@)very shopper will now pay her a dollar less than before, so demand drops by the whole dollar.
    draw tax_demand_curve label_D_tax

B: Shoppers pay four (@erase label_Pstar@) fifty in all, and Mia keeps three fifty.
    draw tax_equilibrium_point tax_guide_lines price_buyers_point label_Pb price_sellers_point label_Ps readout

Exactly the numbers we had when Mia paid. So who hands it over really doesn't matter?
    highlight readout effect box

B: Not in this model. The law picks who writes the cheque, not who bears the cost.
    highlight tax_demand_curve label_D_tax

Then what does pick it?
    highlight demand_curve supply_curve

B: Who can walk away more easily. Here buyers and Mia are equally flexible, so they split it.
    highlight price_buyers_point price_sellers_point effect pulse

B: But bread is a staple. Say shoppers bar (@animate demand.elasticity 0.312 duration 3 ghost true@)ely cut back when its price goes up: their demand is much less elastic.

Now shoppers pay about eighty cents of the dollar, and Mia loses only about twenty.
    highlight readout effect box

B: The side that can't walk away gets stuck with the bill.
    highlight demand_curve label_D

B: Pause and turn Mia's supply curve steep, as if she had nowhere else to sell, and watch the bill move to her.
    explore

So the dollar lands on whoever has fewer ways out.
    highlight label_Pb label_Ps

    pause 0.5

## Does the law ever matter?
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Loaves a week (Q)
    y_label: Price of a loaf (P)
    demand:
      elasticity: 1
    supply:
      elasticity: 1
    equilibrium:
      show: false
    tax:
      amount: 12.5
      side: seller
      label: S + tax
    units:
      price: [0, 8]
      quantity: [0, 1000]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: loaves
sources:
    - id: chetty2009
      title: 'Salience and Taxation: Theory and Evidence'
      authors: Chetty, R., Looney, A. & Kroft, K.
      year: 2009
      finding: In a grocery-store experiment, posting tax-inclusive prices on shelf tags cut demand for the treated products by about 8 percent, though the tax itself was unchanged
      doi: 10.1257/aer.99.4.1145
    - id: saez2019
      title: 'Payroll Taxes, Firm Behavior, and Rent Sharing: Evidence from a Young Workers'' Tax Cut in Sweden'
      authors: Saez, E., Schoefer, B. & Seim, D.
      year: 2019
      finding: A large cut in employer payroll taxes for young workers (2007–2009) had no effect on their net wages relative to slightly older workers, even in the medium run; youth employment rose 2–3 points
      doi: 10.1257/aer.20171937
    - id: gruber1997
      title: 'The Incidence of Payroll Taxation: Evidence from Chile'
      authors: Gruber, J.
      year: 1997
      finding: After Chile's 1981 pension privatisation sharply cut employers' payroll taxes, the cut was fully passed on to workers as higher wages, with no effect on employment
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w5053

    portrait chetty of "Raj Chetty" cameo true hidden true
    scratch tag center cites ["chetty2009"] work ["same tax,","now on the shelf tag:","sales −8%"] font_size 36 hidden true
    scratch cases center cites ["saez2019","gruber1997"] work ["employer tax cut","Sweden: wages flat","Chile: wages up"] font_size 36 hidden true
    scratch sure center work ["less elastic side pays more:","consensus","who hands it over:","mostly agreed long run","contested short run"] font_size 36 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch math center work [{"tex":"\\text{buyers' share} = \\frac{\\varepsilon_S}{\\varepsilon_S + \\varepsilon_D}"},"both = 1:  1 ÷ 2 = 0.5","demand = 0.25:  1 ÷ 1.25 = 0.8"] font_size 38 hidden true

    card title "Does the law ever matter?"

B: Here's my doubt. The model says it never matters who  (@draw tax_equilibrium_point tax_guide_lines price_buyers_point label_Pb price_sellers_point label_Ps@)hands the tax over. Does that survive real shoppers?
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S tax_supply_curve label_S_tax

Not al (@fade axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S tax_supply_curve label_S_tax tax_equilibrium_point tax_guide_lines price_buyers_point label_Pb price_sellers_point label_Ps to 0.25@)ways. In 2009 Raj Chetty and (@draw chetty@) two colleagues put tax-inclusive prices on the shelf tags in a grocery store.

    erase chetty

The tax was  (@draw tag_box tag_line_1@)the same as ever; it had always been (@draw tag_line_2@) added at the till. Yet sales of those goods fell by about eight pe (@draw tag_line_3 ends@)rcent.

B: So a tax people don't see bites less. Where it shows up can matter, even when who pays it shouldn't.
    highlight tag_line_3

    erase tag

And wages can be slow to adjust. When Swede (@draw cases_box cases_line_1@)n cut employers' payroll tax for young workers, the young workers' pay didn't r (@draw cases_line_2 ends@)ise, even years later.

B: Whereas in Chile, a b (@draw cases_line_3@)ig cut in employers' payroll tax ended up in higher wages, just as the model says.

    erase cases

How sure are we? That the less fl (@draw sure_box sure_line_1 sure_line_2@)exible side bears more: consensus. That who hands it over  (@draw sure_line_3 sure_line_4 sure_line_5@)doesn't matter: mostly agreed in the long run, contested in the short run.

    erase sure

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the formula behind the split, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want the formula behind the split?

@math

    erase mathq

Buyers' share of the tax is the supply elasticity divided by both elasticities added together.
    draw math_box math_line_1

Both at one gives bu (@draw math_line_2@)yers a half. Demand at a quarter gives one ov (@draw math_line_3@)er one and a quarter: four fifths, the eighty cents of the bread.

    erase math

@test

    erase mathq

    quiz "Smokers barely cut back when cigarettes cost more; tobacco firms can easily sell elsewhere. Who bears most of a cigarette tax?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: Smokers: they have fewer ways out, so the price they pay rises by most of the tax.
        wrong: Which side has fewer ways out?
        + Smokers
        * Tobacco firms
        * Split evenly

    cards rank ends ["buyers bear most","buyers bear least"]
        * Insulin
        * Petrol, this week
        * One brand of cola

    ask "Rank these taxes by how much of each the buyers bear." on rank stage own store k voice b
        intro: Sellers are equally flexible in all three.
        right: You got {k}. Insulin users can't do without it; cola drinkers just switch brands.
        wrong: You got {k}. How easily can a buyer switch away from each?

    erase rank

    fade axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S tax_supply_curve label_S_tax tax_equilibrium_point tax_guide_lines price_buyers_point label_Pb price_sellers_point label_Ps to 1

    quiz "Parliament moves the whole payroll tax from employers to workers' payslips. Once wages adjust, what does the simple model predict for take-home pay?"
        right: Unchanged: the gap between what the firm pays and what the worker keeps is the same size.
        wrong: Has the gap between what the firm pays and what the worker keeps changed size?
        * Falls by the tax
        + Unchanged
        * Rises

So who really pays a tax? Not whoever the law names, but whoever has fewer ways out. And a tax also takes something from both sides that the council never collects.
    highlight label_Pb label_Ps

    pause 0.8

